        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the protein number without the scoop size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Just Ingredients has 2 g more protein per scoop, but its scoop is 8.5 g heavier. Per gram of powder, Clean Simple Eats is more protein-dense, 74 percent against 62 percent. Compare protein per 100 calories or per gram of powder, not just the headline number on the front of the bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Just Ingredients flavor is NSF certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The NSF registry listed five Just Ingredients flavors when we checked, and French Vanilla was not one of them despite an NSF logo on the bag image. If certification matters to you, search the registry for the exact flavor name before ordering, and check again when the brand reformulates.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating "third-party tested" as a certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clean Simple Eats says its protein is third-party tested, but it names no certifier and publishes no results we could read. That is a brand assurance, not a public program. Drug-tested athletes need a product listed in NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, not a marketing line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the small bag to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 10-serving Clean Simple Eats bag costs about $3.75 per 25 g and the 20-serving bag about $3.12, against $2.71 for the 30-serving bag. Small bags are for testing a flavor. Once you know you like it, the larger bag or a subscription discount brings the per-gram cost down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the collagen in Just Ingredients</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen adds to the protein total on the label but lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, the amino acid most linked to muscle building. The label does not say how much of the 22 g is collagen, so do not compare it gram for gram with a whey-only powder if muscle gain is your goal.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
